-- title:
--   Admissible triples and their rank-two coefficient maximum
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that a real Banach space $X$ with the approximation property is K-convex (the Rademacher projection is bounded on $L_2(\{\pm1\}^n;X)$ uniformly in $n$) if and only if $X$ and its dual $X^*$ both have finite cotype. The converse direction shows that such $X$ has nontrivial type, i.e. does not contain $\ell_1^n$ uniformly, and then derives K-convexity from a power-gap estimate for commuting coordinate projections. Sign matrices are Boolean $m\times m$ matrices $S$; $C_{k,l}(f)$ is the total Fourier coefficient of $f$ over $k\times l$ rectangles, and $T_j$ denotes the Chebyshev polynomial of degree $j$. Fix $m$ and $a,b>0$.
--
--   1. **Scalar triples.** $(\Psi,f,g)$ with $\Psi$ on sign matrices and $f,g$ on $\{0,1\}^m$.
--   2. **Admissible set.** Triples that are scalar admissible for $(a,b)$. It is a compact subset of a finite-dimensional space, defined by finitely many closed linear and bound conditions.
--   3. **Rank-two maximum.**
--   $$
--   \max_{\operatorname{rank}P\ge2}|\hat\Psi(P)| ,
--   $$
--   a continuous function of $\Psi$. By compactness its minimum over the admissible set is attained, so it is a positive constant as soon as it never vanishes.
